Title: LAST ROUND
Credit: Story and direction by
Author: Hung Quach
Source: Screenplay draft by Claude (Cine Lab), from choices made by the author at Gate 1
Draft date: 2026-09-27 — Gate 2, draft 1
Notes: Target running time 2:45 (165 s) before credits. Only IDA speaks. CAS has no dialogue.

# ACT ONE

= Scene 1 — The Round. Dusk. Ida lights her street as she has every night. (0:00–0:30)

EXT. OSTLER STREET - DUSK

[[0:00]] A curved street of brick houses under a violet sky. Twelve gas lamps, all dark. Between them, taller posts with broad frosted panels stand unlit, their wires still new.

IDA MARROW (74), upright, long split-tailed coat, soft felt hat, walks up the street with a short wooden ladder across her right shoulder.

She sets the ladder against the first lamp, climbs three rungs, opens the valve, touches the flame in with her lighting pole.

*Phup.* Amber light blooms. Her shadow falls long and soft across the cobbles.

She holds both palms to the glass. One. Two. Three. Then she tips the flame of her pole into the small tin lantern at her belt, snaps its door shut, and climbs down.

[[0:10]]

IDA
(softly, to the lamp)
Evening, old street.

She takes out a pocket watch, frowns at it, taps it twice with a fingernail, and walks on.

[[0:22]] Lamp after lamp. Behind her the street fills with pools of amber and bands of dark between them. Her shadow swings from one side of her to the other as she passes each post.

[[0:28]] Far away, at the top of the street, a relay CLICKS.

= Scene 2 — Switch-on. The new lights arrive and take her shadow. (0:30–0:55)

EXT. OSTLER STREET - CONTINUOUS

[[0:30]] At the square at the head of the street, the frosted panels flicker twice and stand white. Then the next block. Then the next. A wave of flat white light rolls down the hill toward her with a low electric hum.

Ida, on her ladder at lamp seven, turns.

The white reaches the lamps she lit a minute ago. Their amber pools thin out, then vanish into an even white. The bands of dark between the posts are gone.

[[0:46]] The wave reaches her. She looks down.

Her shadow is gone. Only a faint grey smudge under her boots.

She lifts one hand. Nothing moves on the cobbles.

# ACT TWO

= Scene 3 — The Race. She hurries to light what is left before the white arrives. (0:55–1:25)

EXT. OSTLER STREET - LOWER END - CONTINUOUS

[[0:55]] Ida shoulders the ladder and walks faster. Lamp eight. Lamp nine. Each one blooms amber, throws her shadow long — and a few seconds later the white overtakes it and the shadow is erased again.

She checks her watch against the white wave. Seven minutes. She is late, for the first time in forty years. A dry, small breath of a laugh.

[[1:10]] She climbs lamp ten too fast, fumbles the pole, catches it.

IDA
(under her breath)
Not yet... not yet.

The lamp catches. The white arrives. The amber drowns.

[[1:18]] The last lamp, the eleventh, stands against the lime-white wall of a warehouse at the end of the street. Its electric post is still dark: the last block has not switched over yet. She runs the last few steps, climbs, lights it. Amber. She climbs down, breathing hard, into her own long shadow.

= Scene 4 — The Wall. A boy is making a bird out of her light. The white takes it. She gives it back. (1:25–1:55)

EXT. END OF OSTLER STREET - WAREHOUSE WALL - CONTINUOUS

[[1:25]] Beside the lamp she has just lit, CAS (10), thin, knit cap with a bobble, ears sticking out, stands facing the warehouse wall with both arms raised.

On the wall: a bird. His thumbs locked, his fingers spread, it flaps slowly.

Ida stops. Watches. She does not call out.

[[1:35]] The electric post above them clicks, flickers twice, and stands white. White floods the wall.

The bird fades — grey, then nothing. Cas's hands are still up, flapping at a blank wall. He flaps harder. Nothing.

He lowers his hands. He turns and sees Ida — and the small tin lantern at her belt, still glowing amber.

He looks at the lantern. He does not ask.

[[1:44]] Ida unhooks the lantern, crouches, and holds it close to the wall, a foot from his hands.

IDA
Go on, then.

Cas raises his hands into the little pool of amber. On the wall a bird appears — smaller, warmer — and flies.

Cas lets out a soft laugh.

# ACT THREE

= Scene 5 — The Last Lamp. Ida chooses to put out her own last light and give the flame away. (1:55–2:25)

EXT. END OF OSTLER STREET - CONTINUOUS

[[1:55]] Ida sets the lantern down on the cobbles against the wall and straightens up beside Cas.

On the white wall, thrown by the little lantern, two shadows rise side by side: hers, tall, hat brim and split coat, and his, small, bobble cap, hands still half-raised. The only two shadows in the city.

Ida looks at them for a long moment.

Above them, the eleventh gas lamp still burns — pale now, a weak yellow thumbprint inside the white. Ida climbs the ladder to it. Cas holds the ladder with both hands. Her hand rests on the valve.

[[2:02]]

IDA
(softly, voice catching)
That's the last one, then. Goodnight, old street. You'll be brighter now. Just... keep a little dark for the ones who need it.

She turns the valve. The last gas lamp in the city goes out. Nothing else changes: the white does not even flicker.

[[2:17]] At the bottom of the ladder, she picks up the lantern and holds it out to Cas. He takes it with both hands.

[[2:19]]

IDA
Warm your hands first. Three counts.

[[2:22]] Cas holds his palms to the glass. One. Two. Three.

= Scene 6 — The Window. The street is white. One window is not. (2:25–2:45)

EXT. OSTLER STREET - HIGH ANGLE - NIGHT

[[2:25]] High above the rooftops: the whole city an even, sleepless white. In the middle of it, one small window glows amber.

INT. CAS'S ROOM - NIGHT

The lantern on the sill. On the wall, a large, soft bird opens its wings and flies across the plaster.

EXT. OSTLER STREET - UNDER THE WINDOW - NIGHT

[[2:35]] Below, Ida looks up at the window. In its faint amber spill, a thin shadow of her lies on the cobbles again.

She takes out her pocket watch, turns the crown, and sets it seven minutes forward. Closes it. Puts it away.

She shoulders the ladder and walks out of frame. Her small shadow walks with her until the white takes it.

[[2:45]]

> FADE OUT.

> END
